Local Development
Requirements
•	Node.js 20 or newer
•	pnpm
•	Expo-compatible Android or iOS development environment
•	ESP32 hardware for BLE testing
•	A configured database for backend development
•	A LiveKit Cloud project for live camera testing

Install Dependencies
pnpm install

Configure Environment Variables
Create a local environment file from the example template when available. Server-only LiveKit values must never be placed in the mobile bundle or committed to Git:

LIVEKIT_URL=wss://your-project.livekit.cloud
LIVEKIT_API_KEY=your_server_api_key
LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=your_server_api_secret

The mobile app may use a browser-safe API base URL, but it must not contain the LiveKit API secret.

Start the Development Project
pnpm dev

The managed project uses:

•	Metro/Web preview: http://localhost:8081
•	Backend API: http://localhost:3000

Useful commands:

pnpm check       # TypeScript validation
pnpm lint        # Expo lint checks
pnpm db:push     # Apply the Drizzle schema
pnpm test        # Run the configured test suite

SOS Flow
1	The user presses SOS in the app or triggers the wearable emergency input.
2	The app records the event and requests the freshest available location.
3	The configured SMS flow sends the alert to 8825542451 with a Google Maps location link.
4	The app shows a clear camera-consent prompt.
5	If the user approves, the app requests a short-lived LiveKit publisher token.
6	The mobile app publishes the camera stream to the SOS room.
7	An authenticated trusted viewer requests a short-lived viewer token and joins the room.
8	The user can stop camera sharing at any time, and the session is disconnected when the SOS flow ends.

Privacy and Security
•	Camera access is never started silently; explicit user consent is required.
•	A visible in-app status indicates when live camera sharing is active.
•	LiveKit API credentials remain on the server and are not bundled into Expo output.
•	Publisher and viewer tokens are short-lived and scoped to the SOS room.
•	Viewer access requires authentication.
•	GPS data should be treated as sensitive personal information.
•	Do not commit .env, API secrets, private keys, or production contact lists.
•	Use test phone numbers and test rooms during development.
•	Review local privacy, consent, emergency-contact, and data-retention requirements before deployment.

Hardware Bring-Up
9	Assemble the ESP32 and sensors according to the wiring guide.
10	Flash the selected firmware sketch from firmware/.
11	Confirm power and ground connections before attaching sensors.
12	Test the BLE UART advertisement and pairing.
13	Verify motion, PPG contact, GPS, and battery packets individually.
14	Test the manual SOS input without sending alerts to a real emergency contact.
15	Test the SIM800L path with a controlled test number.
16	Connect the mobile app and confirm event history updates.

The firmware sketches are reference prototypes. Confirm voltage levels, current requirements, antenna placement, and enclosure safety before field use.

